{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VKGxY9NstedfCFF1f6U1V2","type":"tool_result","content":"# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Graphs\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef graph(n, edges):\n    \"\"\"`(n, adj)` from an edge list on vertices `0..n-1`.\"\"\"\n    adj = [0] * n\n    for u, v in edges:\n        if u == v:\n            raise ValueError(\"loops are not graphs here\")\n        adj[u] |= 1 << v\n        adj[v] |= 1 << u\n    return n, tuple(adj)\n\n\ndef edges_of(g):\n    n, adj = g\n    return [(u, v) for u in range(n) for v in range(u + 1, n) if adj[u] >> v & 1]\n\n\ndef from_nx(h):\n    nodes = sorted(h.nodes())\n    idx = {v: i for i, v in enumerate(nodes)}\n    return graph(len(nodes), [(idx[u], idx[v]) for u, v in h.edges()])\n\n\ndef path_graph(n):\n    return graph(n, [(i, i + 1) for i in range(n - 1)])\n\n        masks.append(m)\n    return masks\n\n\ndef vs_of_layout(g, order):\n    \"\"\"Kinnersley p. 346: `max_{1 <= i < |V|} |V_L(i)|`, `V_L(i)` the vertices\n    at positions <= i with a neighbour at a position > i.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    best = 0\n    masks = _prefix_masks(order)\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        pre = masks[i]\n        cnt = sum(1 for v in order[: i + 1] if adj[v] & ~pre)\n        best = max(best, cnt)\n    return best\n\n\ndef vertex_separation(g):\n    n, _ = g\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n    return min(vs_of_layout(g, p) for p in itertools.permutations(range(n)))\n\n\ndef vsg_of_strategy(g, order):\n    \"\"\"Lengauer p. 467: after the i-th pebbling move, the cut is the set of\n    pebble-free vertices adjacent to a pebbled one; the value is the max cut.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    best, peb = 0, 0\n    for v in order:\n        peb |= 1 << v\n        nb = 0\n        for u in range(n):\n            if peb >> u & 1:\n                nb |= adj[u]\n        best = max(best, popcount(nb & ~peb))\n        f[S] = np.maximum(best, cost[S])\n    return int(f[N - 1])\n\n\ndef vs(g) -> int:\n    return vs_subset_dp(g[1])\n\n\ndef mosp_subset_dp(matrix) -> int:\n    \"\"\"MOSP value by DP over the set of produced patterns: the stacks open\n    after producing `S` are the customers with a pattern in `S` and one\n    outside; the value of an order is the max over its prefixes. Nothing\n    here mentions a graph or a layout.\"\"\"\n    rows = [sum(1 << j for j, x in enumerate(r) if x) for r in matrix]\n    m = len(matrix[0]) if matrix else 0\n    if m == 0:\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# the checks\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef atlas(nmax: int):\n    from networkx.generators.atlas import graph_atlas_g\n    return [from_nx(h) for h in graph_atlas_g() if 1 <= h.number_of_nodes() <= nmax]\n\n\ndef all_matrices(rows: int, cols: int):\n    for bits in range(1 << (rows * cols)):\n        yield tuple(tuple(bits >> (r * cols + c) & 1 for c in range(cols)) for r in range(rows))\n\n\ndef run_checks(nmax: int = 7, out_dir: Path = OUT, log=print, blowup3_nmax: int = 5,\n               lengauer_nmax: int = 4, mosp_max_patterns: int = 14, matrices=(3, 4),\n               expansion_max_ports: int = 20, seed: int = 0) -> dict:\n    out_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    t0 = time.time()\n    graphs = atlas(nmax)\n    res: dict = {\"nmax\": nmax, \"graphs\": len(graphs)}\n    fails: dict[str, list] = {k: [] for k in\n                              [\"dp_vs_permutations\", \"cobipartite_double\", \"moehring_double\",\n                               \"moehring_atlas\", \"blowup2\", \"blowup3\", \"expansion_lower\",\n                               \"expansion_subcubic\", \"mosp_graph_to_instance\",\n                               \"mosp_instance_to_graph\", \"lengauer\"]}\n    counts = Counter()\n    excess = Counter()\n\"\"\"Two-ended search: the state lattice of a refutation, forward and backward.\n\nA closing order `c_1 … c_n` of the customers is feasible for \"MOSP ≤ k\"\nexactly when every prefix `S_t = {c_1 … c_t}` satisfies `|N(S_t) ∖ S_t| ≤ k − 1`\n(`satisfiability/customer_search.py`: closing `c` from `S` costs\n`|N[S ∪ c] ∖ S| = |N(S ∪ c) ∖ (S ∪ c)| + 1`). So \"MOSP ≤ k\" is reachability\nfrom `∅` to `V` inside the family `F_k = {S : |N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1}` by single\nadditions. Reversing the production sequence maps closing orders to closing\norders of the same graph (the opening order reversed), so it gives the same\nsearch; the genuinely different direction is building the order from its\n**end**: states are suffixes `T = V ∖ S`, walked from `V` by single removals\nunder the same membership test, `|∂_in(T)| ≤ k − 1`.\n\nThis module enumerates both frontiers exactly and rule-free: `R_f(i)`, the\nsets of size `i` reachable from `∅` inside `F_k`, and `R_b(i)`, those from\nwhich `V` is reachable. A refutation at `k` is `R_f(i) = ∅` for some `i`\n(the forward wall `i_f`), equivalently `R_b(i) = ∅` for some `i` (the\nbackward wall `i_b`), equivalently `R_f(i) ∩ R_b(i) = ∅` at every `i`. The\nforward search's work is `Σ_{i < i_f} |R_f(i)|`, the backward's\n`Σ_{i > i_b} |R_b(i)|`, and a meet at level `i` costs\n`Σ_{j ≤ i} |R_f(j)| + Σ_{j ≥ i} |R_b(j)|`. The checkpoint anatomy\n(`reports/checkpoint_relaxation.md` §5, §7) says the forward search's states\npeak at `0.42 n` while the obstruction sits at `0.5–0.7 n`; this measures\nwhether the other end, or a meet, pays — in states, which is what a search\nwith a frontier store would hold.\n\nFree moves (`N[v] ⊆ N[S]`) are a dominance the forward search applies; the\ncounts here are without any rule on either side, so the two directions are\ncompared like with like. `forward_closed(i)` counts the forward states with\nno free move available, the ones the rule-based search can actually visit.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport csv\nimport json\nimport os\nimport time\nfrom multiprocessing import Pool\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nfrom learning.checkpoint_bound import OUT, pilot_population\nfrom learning.obstruction_ledger import adjacency_from_matrix","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019Nk4Nh4tPgjBdGCo4r8q4U","agent_id":"aa05ea241ff8ca58e","session_id":"bb0818b3-32b2-4335-8fc6-3cdded358983","uuid":"fa14bb82-c6cf-4435-a238-ce7b6fbfb6be","timestamp":"2026-11-04T22:15:52.038Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VKGxY9NstedfCFF1f6U1V2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Refute the §5 proofs"}
