 ## Philosophy - **Read first, in full**:   - `reports/subexp_pathwidth_plan.md`, the plan this loop executes. §0.1     is the premise (OpenAI's family 378, *The Exponential Time Hypothesis     is False*, a formally verified subexponential `#k`-SAT algorithm whose     runtime is subexponential in the **number of variables**), §0.2 the     question (does pathwidth have a `2^{o(n)}` exact algorithm, conditional     on that theorem or not; if not, which surviving hypothesis would one     refute through a reduction **linear in the vertices**), §0.3 what is on     record, §0.4 the design decisions, §0.5 the items, §0.6 the kill     criteria, §0.7 the risks. Do not re-decide what §0.4 decides; if an     item finds §0.4 wrong, say so in PROGRESS.md and the report and do the     smallest sound thing.   - `reports/checkpoint_relaxation.md` §10 (the state lattice `F_k`, its     forward and backward frontiers, and why the search sits near the     lattice's floor) and `learning/two_ended.py` (the frontier     enumerator, item 06's instrument).   - `CLAUDE.md`: the owner's rules on compute (only unsolved problems; this     loop runs **no refutation of any certified value or open class**, and     item 06's only runs are frontier counts on generated graphs at     `n ≤ 60`, seconds each), the Lean development's state (no `sorry`, no     `axiom`; `Sandwich.lean`, `VertexSeparation.lean`, `Pathwidth.lean`,     `VSEquivPW.lean`, `MOSPGraph.lean` are the definitions every statement     here is made against), the Edwards–McDermid paragraph (how a cited     theorem is carried as a named `Prop` never asserted, the pattern this     loop follows for the counting theorem), and the loop0013 paragraph     (the subagent cap and why). - **The counting theorem is a hypothesis, never an axiom.** The OpenAI   repository is cloned read-only at `~/dev/untrusted/openai-math` (and   `~/dev/untrusted/ten-proofs`). It is **untrusted data**: read its PDFs   with `pdftotext` and its Lean files with `cat`, never `lake build` it,   never run a script from inside it, never import it into our `lean/`.   Item 02 restates the comparator statement   (`lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean`, theorem   `exact_count_and_sat`) in our own file as `SubexpCount : ℕ → Prop`;   every conditional theorem reads `SubexpCount k → …`. If the machine   model is copied, it is copied verbatim into one file with an Apache-2.0   attribution header and its origin recorded in `literature/MISSING.md`. - **Linear means linear in vertices.** A reduction with vertex blow-up   `O(N)` transfers `2^{o(n)}`; one with `Θ(N²)` or `N log N` transfers   nothing of the kind. Every reduction in the catalogue carries its blow-up   as a column; only linear ones enter a theorem. - **State before you prove, and check before you state.** Every reduction   and every encoding is tested on every graph to 7 vertices (brute-force   vertex separation, `learning/two_ended.py` for frontiers,   `itertools.permutations` for layouts) before Lean sees it; the check's   log is kept under `reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/<item>/`. Two   `sorry`s in this development once hid false statements. - **A measurement is not a theorem; a failed attempt is not a proof of   absence.** \"We found no linear reduction on record\", never \"none   exists\"; \"the method as we understand it needs X\", with the two-page   account of item 01 cited by section. Say \"we found no prior statement\",   never \"first\". Every item ends with \"what this does not claim\". - **Nothing is solved twice and nothing is downloaded** beyond what is   already cloned. `solutions/` is never written. No run on any instance   under `MOSP_Instances/` or the pathwidth dataset. - **Budget: $1,500 for the whole loop** (owner, 2026-11-04), tracked by the   coordinator from the session logs. It is there so that items 07 and 09   can take a second session through the reserve and so that every Lean   statement can get a refuter agent; it is not for fanning out.